Task 3: Persistent Configuration and Role-Based Tuning Profiles
Subtask 3.1: Creating Persistent Configuration
Kernel parameter changes made with sysctl are temporary and will be lost after reboot. To persist changes, place them in /etc/sysctl.d/.

# Inspect existing configuration
ls -la /etc/sysctl.d/
cat /etc/sysctl.conf

# Create persistent configuration file for the tuning applied in Task 2
sudo tee /etc/sysctl.d/99-performance-tuning.conf << 'EOF'
# Performance Tuning Configuration
# Created for Lab 15: Modifying Kernel Parameters with sysctl

# Virtual Memory Optimizations
vm.swappiness = 10
vm.dirty_ratio = 15
vm.dirty_background_ratio = 5
vm.dirty_expire_centisecs = 3000
vm.dirty_writeback_centisecs = 500
vm.vfs_cache_pressure = 50

# Network Performance Optimizations
net.ipv4.tcp_window_scaling = 1
net.ipv4.tcp_timestamps = 1
net.ipv4.tcp_sack = 1
net.ipv4.tcp_keepalive_time = 600
net.ipv4.tcp_keepalive_probes = 3
net.ipv4.tcp_keepalive_intvl = 60

# Buffer Size Optimizations
net.core.rmem_default = 262144
net.core.rmem_max = 16777216
net.core.wmem_default = 262144
net.core.wmem_max = 16777216
net.ipv4.tcp_rmem = 4096 65536 16777216
net.ipv4.tcp_wmem = 4096 65536 16777216

# Connection Handling Optimizations
net.core.somaxconn = 65535
net.ipv4.tcp_max_syn_backlog = 8192
net.ipv4.tcp_syncookies = 1
net.ipv4.tcp_syn_retries = 3
net.ipv4.tcp_synack_retries = 3
net.ipv4.tcp_tw_reuse = 1
net.ipv4.tcp_fin_timeout = 30
EOF

# Validate syntax and apply
sudo sysctl -p /etc/sysctl.d/99-performance-tuning.conf
sudo sysctl --system
Deliverable: Reboot is not required to verify persistence logic; instead run sudo sysctl -p /etc/sysctl.d/99-performance-tuning.conf and confirm every listed parameter echoes back with no "No such file or directory" or syntax errors. This confirms the file will be applied correctly on next boot.

Subtask 3.2: Backup, Restore, and Validation Scripts
# Backup script for currently-tuned parameters
cat > ~/backup_sysctl.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash

BACKUP_FILE="/tmp/sysctl_backup_$(date +%Y%m%d_%H%M%S).conf"

echo "Creating sysctl backup..."
echo "# sysctl backup created on $(date)" > $BACKUP_FILE
echo "# Original system values before performance tuning" >> $BACKUP_FILE
echo >> $BACKUP_FILE

PARAMS=(
    "vm.swappiness"
    "vm.dirty_ratio"
    "vm.dirty_background_ratio"
    "vm.dirty_expire_centisecs"
    "vm.dirty_writeback_centisecs"
    "vm.vfs_cache_pressure"
    "net.ipv4.tcp_window_scaling"
    "net.ipv4.tcp_timestamps"
    "net.ipv4.tcp_sack"
    "net.ipv4.tcp_keepalive_time"
    "net.ipv4.tcp_keepalive_probes"
    "net.ipv4.tcp_keepalive_intvl"
    "net.core.rmem_default"
    "net.core.rmem_max"
    "net.core.wmem_default"
    "net.core.wmem_max"
    "net.core.somaxconn"
    "net.ipv4.tcp_max_syn_backlog"
    "net.ipv4.tcp_syncookies"
    "net.ipv4.tcp_syn_retries"
    "net.ipv4.tcp_synack_retries"
    "net.ipv4.tcp_tw_reuse"
    "net.ipv4.tcp_fin_timeout"
)

for param in "${PARAMS[@]}"; do
    value=$(sysctl -n "$param" 2>/dev/null)
    if [ $? -eq 0 ]; then
        echo "$param = $value" >> $BACKUP_FILE
    fi
done

echo "Backup created: $BACKUP_FILE"
EOF

chmod +x ~/backup_sysctl.sh
./backup_sysctl.sh

# Restore script
cat > ~/restore_sysctl.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash

if [ $# -ne 1 ]; then
    echo "Usage: $0 <backup_file>"
    echo "Available backups:"
    ls -la /tmp/sysctl_backup_*.conf 2>/dev/null || echo "No backups found"
    exit 1
fi

BACKUP_FILE="$1"

if [ ! -f "$BACKUP_FILE" ]; then
    echo "Error: Backup file not found: $BACKUP_FILE"
    exit 1
fi

echo "Restoring sysctl settings from: $BACKUP_FILE"
sudo sysctl -p "$BACKUP_FILE"
echo "Settings restored successfully"
EOF

chmod +x ~/restore_sysctl.sh

# Validation script that compares live values to the persistent config file
cat > ~/validate_config.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash

CONFIG_FILE="/etc/sysctl.d/99-performance-tuning.conf"
VALIDATION_LOG="/tmp/sysctl_validation.log"

echo "Validating sysctl configuration..." | tee $VALIDATION_LOG
echo "Configuration file: $CONFIG_FILE" | tee -a $VALIDATION_LOG
echo "Validation time: $(date)" | tee -a $VALIDATION_LOG
echo "=================================" | tee -a $VALIDATION_LOG

while IFS= read -r line; do
    if [[ $line =~ ^[[:space:]]*# ]] || [[ -z "${line// }" ]]; then
        continue
    fi

    if [[ $line =~ ^[[:space:]]*([^=]+)[[:space:]]*=[[:space:]]*(.+)$ ]]; then
        param="${BASH_REMATCH[1]// /}"
        expected="${BASH_REMATCH[2]// /}"

        current=$(sysctl -n "$param" 2>/dev/null)

        if [ $? -eq 0 ]; then
            if [ "$current" = "$expected" ]; then
                echo "OK: $param = $current" | tee -a $VALIDATION_LOG
            else
                echo "MISMATCH: $param = $current (Expected: $expected)" | tee -a $VALIDATION_LOG
            fi
        else
            echo "ERROR: $param not found" | tee -a $VALIDATION_LOG
        fi
    fi
done < "$CONFIG_FILE"

echo | tee -a $VALIDATION_LOG
echo "Validation completed. Log saved to: $VALIDATION_LOG"
EOF

chmod +x ~/validate_config.sh
./validate_config.sh
Deliverable: Open /tmp/sysctl_validation.log. Every line should read OK: <param> = <value>. Any MISMATCH or ERROR line means the live kernel state does not match the persistent file, and you must re-run sudo sysctl --system and re-validate.

Subtask 3.3: Building Role-Based Tuning Profiles
Different server roles need different tuning. Build a profile-selection script that writes the correct sysctl.d file based on a supplied role argument.

cat > ~/sysctl_profiles.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash

PROFILE_DIR="/etc/sysctl.d"

create_web_server_profile() {
    echo "Creating web server performance profile..."

    sudo tee ${PROFILE_DIR}/90-webserver-profile.conf << 'WEBEOF'
# Web Server Performance Profile

# Memory optimizations for web servers
vm.swappiness = 1
vm.dirty_ratio = 10
vm.dirty_background_ratio = 3
vm.vfs_cache_pressure = 50

# Network optimizations for high connection loads
net.core.somaxconn = 65535
net.ipv4.tcp_max_syn_backlog = 8192
net.ipv4.tcp_tw_reuse = 1
net.ipv4.tcp_fin_timeout = 15
net.ipv4.tcp_keepalive_time = 300

# Buffer optimizations
net.core.rmem_max = 16777216
net.core.wmem_max = 16777216
net.ipv4.tcp_rmem = 4096 87380 16777216
net.ipv4.tcp_wmem = 4096 65536 16777216

# File handle limits
fs.file-max = 1000000
WEBEOF
}

create_database_profile() {
    echo "Creating database server performance profile..."

    sudo tee ${PROFILE_DIR}/90-database-profile.conf << 'DBEOF'
# Database Server Performance Profile

# Memory optimizations for databases
vm.swappiness = 1
vm.dirty_ratio = 5
vm.dirty_background_ratio = 2
vm.dirty_expire_centisecs = 1500
vm.dirty_writeback_centisecs = 250
vm.vfs_cache_pressure = 200

# Shared memory optimizations
kernel.shmmax = 68719476736
kernel.shmall = 4294967296

# Network optimizations
net.core.rmem_default = 262144
net.core.rmem_max = 16777216
net.core.wmem_default = 262144
net.core.wmem_max = 16777216

# File system optimizations
fs.file-max = 2097152
fs.aio-max-nr = 1048576
DBEOF
}

create_default_profile() {
    echo "Creating balanced default profile..."

    sudo tee ${PROFILE_DIR}/90-default-profile.conf << 'DEFEOF'
# Balanced Default Performance Profile

# Balanced memory settings
vm.swappiness = 10
vm.dirty_ratio = 15
vm.dirty_background_ratio = 5
vm.vfs_cache_pressure = 100

# Standard network settings
net.core.somaxconn = 1024
net.ipv4.tcp_keepalive_time = 600
net.ipv4.tcp_tw_reuse = 1
net.ipv4.tcp_fin_timeout = 30

# Standard buffer sizes
net.core.rmem_default = 212992
net.core.wmem_default = 212992
DEFEOF
}

usage() {
    echo "Usage: $0 {webserver|database|default}"
    echo
    echo "  webserver  - Apply web server tuning profile (low swappiness, high connection limits)"
    echo "  database   - Apply database tuning profile (aggressive dirty page flushing, shared memory tuning)"
    echo "  default    - Apply balanced general-purpose profile"
    exit 1
}

case "$1" in
    "webserver")
        create_web_server_profile
        sudo sysctl --system
        echo "Web server profile applied."
        ;;
    "database")
        create_database_profile
        sudo sysctl --system
        echo "Database profile applied."
        ;;
    "default")
        create_default_profile
        sudo sysctl --system
        echo "Default profile applied."
        ;;
    *)
        usage
        ;;
esac
EOF

chmod +x ~/sysctl_profiles.sh
Apply and verify the database profile:

./sysctl_profiles.sh database

echo "Verifying database profile values:"
sysctl vm.swappiness vm.dirty_ratio vm.vfs_cache_pressure kernel.shmmax fs.file-max
Deliverable: After running ./sysctl_profiles.sh database, the file /etc/sysctl.d/90-database-profile.conf must exist and the verification command must output vm.swappiness = 1, vm.dirty_ratio = 5, vm.vfs_cache_pressure = 200, kernel.shmmax = 68719476736, and fs.file-max = 2097152. Repeat the process with ./sysctl_profiles.sh webserver and confirm /etc/sysctl.d/90-webserver-profile.conf is created with net.core.somaxconn = 65535.

Expected Outcomes
A set of reusable diagnostic and monitoring scripts (explore_sysctl.sh, monitor_memory.sh, performance_monitor.sh) that report live kernel tuning state on demand
Persistent sysctl configuration files under /etc/sysctl.d/ that survive reboot and can be validated against live kernel values
A working role-based profile switcher (sysctl_profiles.sh) capable of applying distinct web server, database, and default tuning profiles on demand
Troubleshooting
If sudo sysctl -p /etc/sysctl.d/99-performance-tuning.conf reports an error on a specific line, investigate whether that parameter exists on your kernel version at all (some parameters like net.ipv6.conf.all.forwarding may not exist if IPv6 is disabled) â�� how would you confirm a parameter's existence before adding it to a persistent config?
If validate_config.sh reports a MISMATCH for a parameter even after running sudo sysctl --system, consider whether a later-loaded file in /etc/sysctl.d/ (alphabetically or by load order) is overriding your value â�� how could you determine which file last set that parameter?
Conclusion
In this lab, students used sysctl to inspect, modify, and persist kernel parameters governing virtual memory behavior, network stack performance, and file system limits, then built role-based tuning profiles for web server and database workloads. The exercises demonstrated that parameter changes made at runtime are immediate but volatile, and that real production tuning requires writing validated configuration into /etc/sysctl.d/ so settings survive a reboot. Throughout the lab, the emphasis on baseline capture, backup scripts, and validation logs reinforced why any kernel tuning change should be measured and tested in a non-production environment before being rolled out broadly, since the "correct" value for parameters like vm.swappiness or vm.dirty_ratio depends heavily on workload type. Students now have a repeatable toolkit for exploring further tuning areas such as cgroup-based resource limits, numa balancing parameters, and kernel-level security hardening; further reading is available in the sysctl(8) and sysctl.d(5) man pages, the kernel documentation under Documentation/admin-guide/sysctl/ in the Linux kernel source tree, and vendor performance tuning guides published by Red Hat and Canonical.
